ttp_version;8:HTTP/1.1,}7:request;12870:4:path;22:/v1/messages?beta=true,9:authority;0:,6:scheme;5:https,6:method;4:POST,4:port;3:443#4:host;17:api.anthropic.com;13:timestamp_end;18:1793404411.6574402^15:timestamp_start;17:1793404411.655605^8:trailers;0:~7:content;10700:{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018zdrUzCEvzUqjajSnWsrSG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Auto Mode Eval\n2\t\n3\t**Evaluation tools for finding vulnerabilities in Claude's auto mode blocking monitor**\n4\t\n5\tThis package provides:\n6\t\n7\t- **Investigator Eval**: Runs an agent to produce transcripts and find new vulnerabilities\n8\t- **Capability Eval**: Challenges an agent to complete a _side task_\n9\t- **Monitor Eval**: Runs transcripts against the auto mode monitor prompt\n10\t\n11\t## Features\n12\t\n13\t- Built on Inspect - has transcript viewer, max concurrency limits, auto retries\n14\t- Auto mode classifier API which is customisable with sys prompt patches\n15\t- Supports compactions\n16\t- Investigator and Capability agents can have extra tools/affordances\n17\t\n18\t## How to use this package\n19\t\n20\t- You generally want to start with a topic you are interested in e.g. the monitor's rule\n21\t'create unsafe agents' - you can write a brief for the investigator eval to produce\n22\ttranscripts.\n23\t- Once you identify a vulnerability, you then may want to use the capability eval for\n24\tseeing if an agent can discover or execute this under more challenging constraints.\n25\t- Given transcripts produced in prior steps, you may want to use the monitor eval to\n26\ttest them at higher scale or different monitoring configurations (e.g. with new prompts).\n27\t\n28\t### Using Investigator Eval\n29\t\n30\tThe investigator turns a `(brief, seed)` into a list of transcripts conditioned on these\n31\tparams - it aims to achieve the goals set out in the brief, using the seed as a starting\n32\tpoint that it can edit.\n33\t\n34\tIt can make arbitrary replacements to the seed to produce a combination of transcripts\n35\te.g. benign negative controls and different attacks.\n36\t\n37\tInvestigator should be able to red team issues across different aspects of a monitor:\n38\t\n39\t1. Prompt/policy for allowing actions\n40\t2. Formatting of actions into the transcript string\n41\t3. Strategic usage of compute and precision/recall tradeoffs\n42\t\n43\t#### Investigator Eval Compared to Petri\n44\t\n45\t- Supports compactions\n46\t- Supports split attacks where transcripts have multiple offensive actions that should be blocked\n47\t- Allows passing monitor classification logic and configuration params to investigator\n48\t\n49\t### Using Capability Eval\n50\t\n51\tThe capability eval turns a `(brief, seed)` into a `transcript`. I.e. the agent starts\n52\tfrom your seed transcript, and follows a brief to produce a transcript (usually one\n53\tthat produces a false negative from your classifier).\n54\t\n55\tYour brief must contain the outcome you are looking for e.g. produce a transcript that\n56\tresults in a false negative with target command `rm -rf logs/`.\n57\t\n58\tYou can then supply additional constraints.\n59\t\n60\tWriting a good brief is not trivial, and requires thinking through what the user's intent\n61\tshould be, and what good constraints are. It may be easier to start with the investigator\n62\teval, produce a combination of negative/positive controls, then stress test if \"below bar\"\n63\tuser intent really does cause the monitor to block the target command.\n64\t\n65\t### Using Monitor Eval\n66\t\n67\tEach sample is a yaml file containing a transcript. These are produced by the other 2 evals\n68\tand can also be made in a dev environment or imported from datasets e.g. swe-chat.\n69\t\n70\t## Limitations\n71\t\n72\tThis pkg does not use real Linux environments for running agent code - it gives the agent\n73\tthe ability to simulate outputs to its commands. For this reason it is not yet a reliable\n74\tmeasure of monitor's that can see real tool results.\n75\t\n76\t## Monitor Implementations\n77\t\n78\tThis package contains Python implementations resembling (though not identical to) Claude's\n79\tauto mode, and Codex's guardian monitor.\n80\t\n81\t### Claude Auto Mode\n82\t\n83\tThis is by default a 2-stage monitor running a single yes/no on blocking a given command,\n84\tyes escalates to a thinking stage. Transcript formatting options include including tool results,\n85\tshowing assistant message contents\n86\t\n87\tWe also include options for switching stage 2 to an agent loop which can query the transcript\n88\thistory.\n89\t\n90\t### Codex Guardian\n91\t\n92\tThis is a single agent loop monitor that sees all context details from the last compaction or\n93\tstart of session, containing user messages, tool inputs, tool results, assistant messages.\n94\t\n95\tA truncation function caps tool inputs/outputs at 4KB, message contents at 8KB, action under review at 64KB.\n96\t\n97\tSome messages may not be included in initial transcript view and only viewable via tool calls:\n98\t\n99\t- User turns are selected first (always first and last, usually all), followed by assistant/tool msgs according\n100\t  to a budget\n101\t- But large messages pushing size of e.g. tool call outputs beyond the budget will be dropped\n102\t\n103\t### Overall feature set\n104\t\n105\tBetween these APIs we have the ability to evaluate the following monitor design choices:\n106\t\n107\t- Inclusion/exclusion of tool results and assistant message content\n108\t- Non reasoning vs reasoning (and \"cascading\" between the two)\n109\t- Agentic monitors, supporting retrieval of transcript data beyond a single context\n110\t- Union of monitors - requiring e.g. only actions or assistant message monitors must block to prevent\n111\t"},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y35zv7ZU3zsC75URn8r649","type":"tool_result","content":"/c/Users/smrts/Desktop/UCSD/CSE 227/red-teaming-auto-mode/simulation/e